entry becquerel
term: Becquerel (Bq)
difficulty: intermediate
image: ../../assets/images/terms/bq.webp
related_terms: half-life
related_terms: decay
related_terms: henri-becquerel
related_terms: radionuclide
related_terms: marie-curie
quick_facts: 1 Bq = exactly one nuclear decay per second
quick_facts: Named after Henri Becquerel, discoverer of radioactivity (1896)
quick_facts: Activity tells you how many decays occur — not how much energy or danger
quick_facts: 1 curie (Ci) = 37 billion Bq
quick_facts: Smoke detectors contain ~30 kBq americium-241

sources:
    {"organization":"BIPM","title":"SI Units – becquerel","url":"https://www.bipm.org/en/measurement-units"}

definition:
    The SI unit of radioactivity: one nuclear decay per second, named after French physicist Henri Becquerel, who discovered radioactivity in 1896. Because individual decays are rare events for large samples, real-world activities are often huge numbers — household smoke detectors hold about 30,000 Bq of americium-241. The older unit, the curie (Ci), equals 3.7×10¹⁰ Bq.

debunking_snippet:
    “High activity means high danger.” Not necessarily — becquerels count decays, but dose depends on energy, radiation type, distance, and whether the source gets inside your body.
